AN OVERVIEW OF THE CHALLENGES AHEAD:

The person in this role defines and advances the strategy for electronic component selection, standardization and lifecycle management. They contribute directly to the robustness of our architectures, the resilience of our supply chain and the long-term sustainability of products designed for demanding environments.

Key Responsibilities

• Define and lead the component selection strategy based on technical, commercial, availability and product lifecycle requirements;

• Assess and communicate component-related risks for projects under development;

• Build and maintain strategic relationships with component manufacturers and distributors to anticipate market developments and guide product design;

• Advise and influence design teams on component, architecture and technology choices;

• Serve as the strategic interface between R&D and supply chain stakeholders through a Design for Supply Chain approach;

• Guide design decisions during technical reviews to increase standardization, reuse and platform robustness;

• Optimize engineering bills of materials (BOMs) to minimize supply risks and support product competitiveness;

• Define dual-sourcing strategies and lead the identification and qualification of alternative sources for products in development;

• Support operational continuity through component lifecycle and obsolescence management;

• Lead the creation of new parts in ERP and eCAD systems;

• Implement and continuously improve the methods, metrics and tools required for effective, data-driven management;

• Contribute to strategic initiatives that improve design robustness, component governance and supply chain resilience;

• All other related tasks.

YOU WILL IMPRESS US IF YOU HAVE:

• Education: Bachelor’s degree in electrical engineering or equivalent experience in electronics;

• Experience: At least five years of relevant experience in electronic component engineering or lifecycle management;

• Knowledge: Strong understanding of electronic design, bills of materials, materials requirements planning (MRP), Design for Supply Chain, and technical, commercial and supply risks;

• Profile: Demonstrated ability to analyze complex data, develop recommendations and influence cross-functional decisions without direct reporting authority, excellent communication skills and the ability to collaborate effectively with R&D, Procurement, Supply Chain, Operations, Quality and external partners; attention to detail, ability to manage multiple tasks simultaneously, desire to learn;

• Assets: Knowledge of Siemens Xpedition (Mentor Graphics) and SAP (or another ERP system), proficiency in Microsoft Excel, Python programming and cable design, experience in a regulated environment or with long-lifecycle products;

• Languages: Professional fluency in spoken and written French and English, given regular discussions with suppliers and customers outside Québec.

HOW ABOUT AN EXCEPTIONAL WORK ENVIRONMENT INCLUDING:

• A competitive salary;

• A generous vacation policy and a large number of personal days;

• An advantageous collective insurance program, including telemedecine;

• A collective RRSP program in which Kontron Canada contributes;

• Good balance between work-family-personal life;

• Social and sports activities, including access to a volleyball and soccer field;

• Various training for your professional development;

• A stimulating career path in various sectors of activity;

• Dedicated days for individual and team innovation projects;

• A modern facility with state of the art equipment;

• And much more…

